    "turn_text": "think it's this great division and great You know, partisanship, certainly in Washington. Scott, what we've tried to do in here, in Ohio, what I've tried to do since I've been governors, try to pull people together. And, you know, I'm very proud to say we passed our budget with a bipartisan, in a bipartisan way. We had Democrats, Republicans vote for it. We focused on early childhood education, early childhood development. We focused on getting the money to the school so they could do what we call wellness dollars, wraparound services, which is basically to try to help kids deal with some of the deficiencies that they might have because of trauma or other things. Drug epidemic is so bad. We've got kids who are living in homes that you know, one or both parents are drug addicts. And so trying to compensate for that. But these are things that, you know, I think Democrats and Republicans can agree on. And we did. We got it passed, as I said. We presented a budget to the legislature. They passed it. They made some changes. But We worked things out, it was a good budget, and it's a budget that really invests in our people, which is I think what we need to do. So we've been able to do that in Ohio, and I think that's what people want us to do. I mean, one of the things that, you know, when I go out and talk with people, they just say, you know, Just get stuff done and make things happen and try to make it a better, a better society",
