  {
    "episode_id": 71773,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71773.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "I mean, we've had a balance in this state, really, and I think it's the right thing to do. School choice was started a number of years ago to try to give poor kids who were in tough schools or schools where they just weren't getting the education that they needed the same opportunity that more fluent families had and so that they could get out of that situation. We also, though, have our responsibility to make sure our public schools can function and that we don't pass any kind of legislation that makes it very difficult for these public schools to do what they do. I mean, most people who are listening to you today probably like their public schools, not everybody, I understand, but we live in a Fran and I live in a small community. We think our public schools are very good public school. What we're trying to do is school choice and what we've been trying to do is school choice is to reach those kids who aren't in that situation and who don't have the money to get out of it and try to give them some ability to go and make some choices. And it's a balance that we try to",
