    "turn_text": "of opportunities zones are working progress, but we think it's going to make a difference. We've kind of doubled down on that in Ohio. We took the federal law and we added to an economic incentive and taxes break. for people who invest in these opportunities zones in the state of Ohio, which are generally in more poor areas, and we're trying to create opportunity for people. But I don't think we ever had the luxury of saying, oh, this kid's born Appalachia. We don't need to worry about him or this kid's in the inner city. That never was right. But today, it's vitally important, not just for that child and that family, but it's vitally important for the future of the state that we don't waste anybody and that everybody can live up to their potential. And if that's going to college, if that's the pathway that that person, a kid wants to go down, that's fine. But the pathway is a career in the trades, a career in using your hands. We ought to be there to help that young person achieve that goal that they have. You know, I know with our own kids what you want for your own kids is that they find something that they have a passion for and they get excited about and they're good at and they can do and live a decent life. And that's what we should want for all of our kids. And I think we're making some progress. I think, you know, we've got some career centers in Ohio that are doing a very, very good job. We've got some of our two-year colleges or community colleges that are doing a good job. I think we're moving forward in this",
