  {
    "episode_id": 71773,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71773.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 85,
    "turn_text": "Well, this is something I've been very interested in going back to when I was a county prosecuting attorney in southwest Ohio and Green County. I represented children's services and I saw some horrible cases of abuse and so I've always been very Interested in this and so I put together Shortly after I became governor a group to look at the foster care system and see what we can do to improve our foster care system so that we can get kids Into permanent homes quicker so that we can You know, help the caseworkers who are out there who many times are not only overworked, but they've got a huge caseload and it's very difficult for them to keep up with every single child that they're supposed to be keeping track of. And so I put a group together. They went around the state. They held 10 different hearings. They heard from, I think, about 400 people in testimony or in written testimony. And they're going to come back with some recommendations. They recommended they came back with a few recommendations today, which is up on our webpage. If anyone wants to take a look at the governor's webpage, I'll just highlight maybe one or two. One is that it's important, I think, for the State of Ohio to establish some uniformity as far as training and pay for that training so that our case workers, people who are out there dealing with very difficult situations, have the training that they need and get that continuous training every single year. We need to take that on. We need to pay for it. We need to say that that's the state's responsibility. Two, I think I would also mention one of the recommendations was that foster parents Let's take a case where foster parents get a child and the child is two days old, get it from mom for whatever reason. Maybe she's a drug addict, whatever, she can't take care of the child. There's no one else that goes to the foster parents. And we've all seen these horror stories where the foster parents have that kid for a few years. And then that child was taken away from them. It's the only parent that that child has known. They want to adopt the child, but then the child is put back in, you know, some relatives home. And so we never should lose sight of the fact that we should be interested in the most important thing is the safety of the child. Most important thing is the welfare of that child. And taking that child out of a home with that child, it's the only parents that child is known. It's just not a good thing for that child. I mean, that's So that's something that was a recommendation and one of the recommendations was that foster parents who've had the child for a significant period of time should be considered at least on equal footing with maybe an aunt who comes in who maybe has not been in that child's life before. The current law today favors that aunt who may be very well intended, but she may not know that child or may not have that relationship with the child. So we want to get back to the point where judges can make decisions and try to make decisions in the totally and the best interest of that kid and the best interest of that child.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
